lib/ebooks/understandingoeb/chapter4.html:274:<p>Block elements could be considered the opposite of inline elements. They are the enclosing elements within which inline elements are placed. That is, inline elements have to have some element to be inside; this element is ultimately a block element (although inline elements can appear inside other inline elements). You've already seen one example of a block element, the <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> element representing a paragraph. Block elements could also be classified as elements that automatically have a line break before and after them; they might therefore also be appropriately called "out-of-line" elements.</p>
